1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,600
Memory is expensive?
1 KB is enough for everyone!

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,300
Hello, friends and colleagues!

3
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:13,650
I've made a little website for my
experiments with sound and music.

4
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:23,910
It's hosted on GitHub Pages.

5
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:29,680
The site is served directly
from the source files.

6
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:40,180
The main result: I managed to fit
music into a single kilobyte.

7
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:42,880
Fairly long tunes, too.

8
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:50,720
Even with sample-like sounds
that resemble real instruments.

9
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,280
Here's a separate page on the site.

10
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:06,570
It emulates an ATtiny13.

11
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:11,210
There's even a stereo version:

12
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:16,320
it has two PWM outputs

13
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:19,360
that can run at the same time.

14
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,640
Let's listen to a little of it.

15
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:21,120
It stores the melody and the samples
in a very compact format.

16
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:30,440
Output uses pulse-width modulation,
as I mentioned.

17
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:37,020
I've also recently added
support for the ATtiny88.

18
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,240
That's it here. Back to the home page.

19
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:54,220
Speech: I also tried fitting that
into the same ATtiny13.

20
00:02:56,950 --> 00:03:03,510
Getting good-quality speech into it
is very, very difficult.

21
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:14,400
This original is from
the Kitten Named Woof cartoon.

22
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:23,910
This one's from Winnie-the-Pooh.

23
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:27,550
That longer Winnie-the-Pooh clip
comes out the worst.

24
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:30,850
It also has background noise:
him knocking at the door.

25
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:53,920
I did manage to fit a short phrase
about a second long.

26
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:00,920
It's certainly not perfect,
but there's probably more we can do.

27
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:11,590
Someone in a chat built
an Intel 8080 processor tester.

28
00:04:11,670 --> 00:04:16,950
It checks whether the processor
is working properly.

29
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:22,980
I came up with a small piece
of firmware for this emulator, too.

30
00:04:25,290 --> 00:04:32,460
The firmware first checks
all the connected ROM.

31
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:39,160
Just like my Z80 Tester,
there's no RAM here.

32
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:45,110
But there is ROM: a 64 KB chip.

33
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:48,150
Only 32 KB of it is actually connected.

34
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:52,370
I tried to fit something
into those 32 KB.

35
00:04:53,170 --> 00:04:59,310
First, a dual CRC check
using two polynomials.

36
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:05,530
It checks all the ROM that's available.

37
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:10,480
It signals the completion
of each check:

38
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:16,860
a short beep for every 4 KB.

39
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:24,710
Then it tests the instructions
that don't write to memory.

40
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,420
Then three long beeps.

41
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:34,920
I've also added speech.
Let me show you how it works.

42
00:05:44,150 --> 00:05:49,440
It's an old processor, of course.

43
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:55,100
Every instruction takes
at least four clock cycles.

44
00:05:55,460 --> 00:06:02,500
The average is around eight or nine
clock cycles per instruction.

45
00:06:04,170 --> 00:06:06,770
The clock is only 3.5 MHz.

46
00:06:06,770 --> 00:06:14,590
Even that speed isn't supported
by every example of this processor.

47
00:06:17,750 --> 00:06:20,470
I've also tried making it play music.

48
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:38,800
I accidentally stopped the recording.
Let's carry on.

49
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:46,880
All these players and emulators
have a Download firmware button.

50
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:51,040
You can download it and try it
on your own hardware.

51
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,060
Back to the home page.

52
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:03,800
Here's a link to the other project
where my music experiments started.

53
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:11,460
I'm still doing some work on it, too.

54
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:15,380
I experimented with PDM:

55
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,240
pulse-density modulation is quite noisy.

56
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:29,240
It's hard to reach a high enough rate
to keep that noise down.

57
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:39,260
Now I want to try PWM:
pulse-width modulation.

58
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:45,180
It has a carrier tone at
the modulation frequency,

59
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:54,080
but to my ears the sound is better
at lower frequencies.

60
00:07:54,530 --> 00:08:01,070
Here's a PWM example.

61
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,600
It's a little quiet.

62
00:08:25,420 --> 00:08:26,440
There are two tunes.

63
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:31,050
You switch them using INT and NMI:

64
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:34,730
the maskable and non-maskable interrupts.

65
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:52,630
I also experimented with
higher-frequency PDM.

66
00:08:52,630 --> 00:08:57,210
That's pulse-density modulation.

67
00:08:57,210 --> 00:09:04,130
Here, for example, we're aiming
for 384 kHz.

68
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:07,160
Actually, we've already reached it.

69
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,780
There's a filter, so that's
how it sounds.

70
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:21,880
But if we move the filter cutoff
to the edge of the audible range,

71
00:09:22,930 --> 00:09:25,090
the sound changes noticeably.

72
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:49,110
Of course, this uses 64 KB,
not one kilobyte.

73
00:09:49,110 --> 00:09:51,390
You can fit much more into that.

74
00:09:52,090 --> 00:09:58,290
I haven't yet tried porting the format

75
00:09:58,290 --> 00:10:02,880
used on the Atmel ATtiny13.

76
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:09,300
I think it would let us
fit considerably more here

77
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:11,180
if we used the same format.

78
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:20,010
Let's go back to the home page.

79
00:10:21,130 --> 00:10:28,600
There's no link back from this page:
it's a separate site on another domain.

80
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:31,840
Here's my MicroMusic site.

81
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:37,100
There's also an audio codec lab.

82
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:43,680
I experimented with this right in
the ChatGPT mobile app.

83
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:52,220
I asked it to make an audio codec
with really good compression,

84
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,560
and an HTML page
so I could test it.

85
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:01,020
These are those test pages.

86
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:08,190
There are also standard codecs here
so you can compare the results.

87
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:16,730
For example, let's choose a file.

88
00:11:16,730 --> 00:11:24,620
I downloaded some funny cartoon lines
just for fun, as test samples.

89
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:32,100
Let's encode it.

90
00:11:33,830 --> 00:11:40,920
Here we're trying to encode the signal
with a few sine waves and noise.

91
00:11:42,290 --> 00:11:44,410
That's this codec.

92
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:50,040
Overall, it's intelligible.

93
00:11:51,070 --> 00:11:56,610
There's also another synthesizer.

94
00:11:56,850 --> 00:12:03,610
It adjusts the frequency
and tries to find suitable coefficients.

95
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:11,730
It uses a similar principle
to the older Talkie synthesizer.

96
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:19,320
The same principle was used
in Talkie-style speech synthesis.

97
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:24,620
Here's the closest version.
Let's try it now.

98
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:30,030
That's the Talkie-style one.

99
00:12:30,030 --> 00:12:32,290
Here I tried to improve it.

100
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:36,780
Let's encode it and have a listen.

101
00:12:39,310 --> 00:12:41,650
It does sound a little different,

102
00:12:41,650 --> 00:12:46,870
but it's still quite far
from the original.

103
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:56,080
This is the kind of codec
I used for sound on the ATtiny13.

104
00:13:00,830 --> 00:13:07,650
Well, that's everything
I wanted to show you.

105
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:10,040
Thank you all for watching!
